<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sankore Madrasah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sankore mosque-university, Sankore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sankore Madrasah was an Islamic centre of learning based around the Sankore Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>. Together with other mosques and scholarly households in the city it formed a widely known network of teaching in theology, law, astronomy and other subjects. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> present it as a symbol of Timbuktu's flourishing scholarly culture under Songhai rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sankore-madrasah.jpg" alt="Sankore Madrasah" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anne and David · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2007_Sankore_Mosque_Timbuktu_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Sankoré mosque stands in the Sankoré quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, where most of the town's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> lived. It was built in the 14th or early 15th century with the financial backing of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> woman of the Aghlal tribe, and its Sudano-Sahelian architecture differs from that of the mosques of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>. Timbuktu grew as a commercial centre in the 14th century as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walata">Walata</a> declined. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who visited in 1352, said nothing of Islamic learning there, but a century later the Aqit clan of the Massufa Berbers settled in the town with a deep tradition of Islamic law.</p><p>Between 1578 and 1582 the Chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">Qadi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, Imam Al-Aqib ibn Mahmud ibn Umar, demolished the sanctuary and had it rebuilt with the dimensions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. Classes were held in its open courtyard, built of clay and wood beams.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The name University of Sankoré has sometimes been used, but there is no evidence of a central teaching body as in medieval European universities. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> had no central administration, student registers or set course of study. It consisted of individual scholars, each with private students, who usually studied with one teacher for up to ten years. Lessons took place at the teacher's house or at the mosque, and students paid their own way with money or goods rather than from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waqf">waqf</a> endowments.</p><p>Study began at Qur'anic schools, which taught writing and the memorisation of the Qur'an; by the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> had as many as 150 to 180 of them, with an estimated 4,000 to 9,000 pupils. Higher study covered Arabic grammar, jurisprudence, mathematics, astronomy and history, and graduates at each level received a turban. Around 200 to 300 people from wealthy families reached the rank of scholar. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a> visited in 1526 he noted that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> sold for more than any other merchandise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> flourished in the 15th and 16th centuries under the Askia dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>, drawing scholars from as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Ahmad Baba, its last chancellor, wrote over forty books, among them the biographical dictionary Nayl al-ibtihaj. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> defeated the Songhai at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tondibi">Battle of Tondibi</a> in 1591, Moroccan troops arrested many Sankoré scholars in 1594 on charges of sedition and deported them to Morocco with their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. Ahmad Baba lost a library of 1,600 books.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> and its scholars left a lasting mark on the Sudanic Muslim world. The building has since been threatened by urban growth and flood damage, and restoration committees and a planned buffer zone have aimed to protect it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sankoré Madrasah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Leo Africanus, the Granada-born traveller raised in Fez, visited around 1510 and described a wealthy city where books were sold at greater profit than any other merchandise and where scholars gathered around the <b>Sankore</b> mosque.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
